  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 236 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe primary concern beyond the next 24 hours continues to be a \nhighly amplified and progressive long wave trough that will move \nacross the CONUS this week. The 12Z deterministic GFS and ECMWF look \nto be in near lock-step with the synoptic pattern this week. The \ntrough will move across the Rockies tonight and into the Great \nPlains on Monday.  The surface reflection of the trough develops \novernight tonight and then tracks into the Upper Midwest by 06-12Z \nTuesday.  The trough then slows and closes off Tuesday, and then \nopens back up and continues to the east-northeast across the Great \nLakes into New England by next Sunday.  Models show the cold front \nassociated with this system entering northwest Missouri Monday night \nand continuing slowly to the east to near the Mississippi River by \naround 06Z Wednesday.\n\nModels continue to show strong low level moisture convergence ahead \nof the front with anomalously high precipitable water values in the \n1.5 to 2 inch range.  Would normally be concerned for potential \nflooding in this situation with the slow movement of the front and \nhigh p-wats, but antecedent dryness and low flow on most area streams \nand rivers largely mitigates the flooding risk.  Additionally, \nguidance suggests there will be very little instability for \nconvection to tap into.  What little there is will probably produce \nsome thunderstorms, but BUFKIT soundings indicate the storms will be \nmoving quickly enough that long-durration heavy rain is not likely, \nand CAMs that go out that far agree.  While some locations could get \nmore than one or two rounds of moderate to heavy rain, flooding does \nnot look likely due to the aforementioned conditions.  Severe \nthunderstorms also look unlikely due to the lack of instability \ndespite deep-layer shear between 45-65kts.  However the threat for \nsevere storms should be monitored closely as breaks in the overcast \non Tuesday would allow for more heating than is presently forecast \nand therefore greater instability.\n\nRain chances end from west to east on Wednesday as the surface front \nclears the area and drier air moves into the Mid Mississippi Valley. \n The airmass behind the front will be drier and noticeably cooler, \nbut temperatures are forecast to remain near to a degree or two \nabove normal through the end of the forecast period.  The next \nchance for accumulating rainfall is Saturday night into Sunday as \nanother strong trough moves into the Midwest.  While this system is \nstill far out in the future, initial indications are that it will \nnot produce hazardous weather.",
